# TypeScript — Zero Type Errors

## Non-negotiable

- Never ship code with TypeScript errors.
- Run `npx tsc --noEmit` (or `npm run build`) before marking a step complete.
- Do not use `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error`, or `any` to silence errors unless documented with a one-line reason.

## Fix errors the correct way

| ❌ Wrong | ✅ Correct |
|---|---|
| `as any` to bypass | Narrow the type or add a proper type guard |
| `@ts-ignore` | Fix the underlying type mismatch |
| Optional chaining everywhere | Define accurate types at boundaries (API, props, state) |
| Duplicate types | Import shared types from `types/` or co-locate with domain |
| `// @ts-nocheck` | Never use in production code |

## Patterns

- API responses (Manus, etc.): define Zod schema or explicit interface at the boundary, parse once, use typed result internally.
- React props: explicit `interface` or `type`; no implicit `any` on event handlers.
- Union types: use exhaustive `switch` with `never` default.
- Nullable values: handle `null`/`undefined` explicitly; don't assume.

## Before committing

- [ ] `npx tsc --noEmit` passes
- [ ] `npm run build` passes (once Next.js exists)
- [ ] No new `@ts-ignore` or untyped `any`
